# meta::dateModified = 2014-04-02
# meta::genre = academic
# meta::salientEntities = 48 (5*), 51 (5*), 44 (4*), 107 (4*), 53 (3*), 89 (3), 84 (2), 90 (2), 92 (2), 106 (2*), 141 (2*), 5 (1), 36 (1), 37 (1), 39 (1), 45 (1), 49 (1), 50 (1), 52 (1), 61 (1), 62 (1), 63 (1), 91 (1), 93 (1), 102 (1), 104 (1*), 140 (1*), 151 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0078273
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Using literature on 31 languages and recorded data from 10 languages, this paper argues that 'huh' is a universal word used across languages by listeners to initiate repair in conversation when they do not understand speakers.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Research comparing 10 spoken languages from 5 continents reveals that “Huh?” is likely a universal word that functions as a repair initiator in conversations across languages, challenging the linguistic principle that word sounds are arbitrary and culturally specific.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) This study presents evidence that the interjection “Huh?” is a universal word across diverse languages, functioning as a linguistic tool for signaling problems in conversation, with research demonstrating its consistent form and use for other-initiated repair across 31 languages, suggesting its form is influenced by universal conversational needs and pressures.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Researchers have found evidence that the word “Huh?” is a universal word, used in many languages to signal problems of hearing and understanding in conversations, and that its form is remarkably similar across languages.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) This study examines the universality and linguistic nature of the interjection “Huh?” across various languages, finding strong similarities in both form and function, suggesting it may be a universal word used universally to signal comprehension failures during communication.
# meta::title = Is “Huh?” a Universal Word? Conversational Infrastructure and the Convergent Evolution of Linguistic Items
