# meta::genre = academic
# meta::salientEntities = 3 (5*), 129 (5*), 8 (4*), 32 (4), 4 (3), 41 (3), 143 (3), 7 (2), 137 (2*), 140 (2), 142 (2*), 150 (2), 1 (1*), 21 (1), 141 (1*), 144 (1), 145 (1), 152 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://journals.linguisticsociety.org/proceedings/index.php/PLSA/article/view/4112/3810
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This discussion concludes based on experimental results that Chitonga speaking children acquire pragmatic competence in Scalar Implicature (SI) in the order known from previous studies.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) This study investigated scalar implicature interpretation in Chitonga-speaking children over age six, finding that while pragmatic inference acquisition follows previously identified patterns, it appears to be completed at a later age in this language compared to other studied languages.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) This study finds that Chitonga-speaking children over six show adult-like scalar implicature (SI) interpretations mainly with cardinal numbers, though other conditions varied and results suggest delayed SI acquisition in Chitonga due to language-specific or methodological factors, with recommendations for larger, culturally informed studies to further explore these findings.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) This study examines the scalar interpretation (SI) abilities of Chitonga-speaking children aged 6 and above, and finds that their pragmatic inference acquisition follows a similar pattern to previous research, but appears to be completed at a later age in this language.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) A study examined how Chitonga-speaking children interpret speech acts, finding that while they followed expected patterns of difficulty, they acquired adult-like interpretations later than previously seen, suggesting language-specific characteristics influence this process.
# meta::title = Scalar implicature in Chitonga-speaking children
