# newdoc id = GUM_academic_theropod
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Bruno Grossi, José Iriarte-Díaz, Omar Larach, Mauricio Canals, Rodrigo A. Vásquez
# meta::dateCollected = 2019-09-11
# meta::dateCreated = 2014-02-05
# meta::dateModified = 2014-02-05
# meta::genre = academic
# meta::salientEntities = 4 (5*), 7 (4), 8 (4*), 9 (4*), 11 (4), 16 (4*), 45 (3*), 2 (2), 10 (2*), 17 (2), 27 (2), 30 (2), 88 (2*), 1 (1*), 6 (1), 26 (1*), 34 (1*), 44 (1*), 82 (1), 101 (1), 113 (1), 154 (1), 193 (1), 209 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0088458
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This discussion summarizes the results of an experiment in which an artificial tail was affixed to birds' bodies in order to study possible ways in which bipedal non-avian dinosaurs may have walked, finding that the location of the CoM may be a key factor in defining limb posture and kinematics.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) When researchers added artificial tails to chickens during development, they observed changes in posture and movement patterns that resembled those of non-avian dinosaurs, with more vertical femurs and hip-driven locomotion rather than the knee-driven locomotion typical of modern birds.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) This study demonstrates that attaching an artificial tail to chickens can shift their center of mass and alter their locomotion to more closely resemble that of non-avian dinosaurs, thereby offering insights into dinosaur biomechanics and evolution, though the method primarily accounts for tail influences rather than forelimb factors in such shifts.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Researchers used an artificial tail to study the effects of a more vertically oriented femur on the locomotion of chickens, finding that the change in femur orientation was associated with a more hip-driven gait and reduced bending and torsional moments, suggesting that non-avian dinosaurs may have had similar limb postures and kinematics.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) An artificial tail added to chickens altered their posture and gait, resembling that of non-avian dinosaurs, highlighting the significant impact of center of mass location on limb biomechanics and suggesting that such experiments can aid in understanding dinosaur locomotion.
# meta::title = Walking Like Dinosaurs: Chickens with Artificial Tails Provide Clues about Non-Avian Theropod Locomotion
# newpar
# newpar_block = head rend:::"bold" (1 s)
# sent_id = GUM_academic_theropod-1
# s_type = frag
# s_prominence = 2
# transition = establishment
# text = Discussion
