# meta::genre = academic
# meta::salientEntities = 6 (5*), 54 (5*), 90 (5*), 4 (4*), 96 (4*), 2 (3), 3 (3), 5 (3), 21 (2), 51 (2*), 56 (2), 61 (2), 85 (2), 111 (2*), 113 (2*), 1 (1), 20 (1), 55 (1), 57 (1), 62 (1), 71 (1), 82 (1), 83 (1), 93 (1*), 97 (1*), 98 (1*), 117 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://dh2017.adho.org/abstracts/046/046.pdf
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This paper studies the responses of viewers of the series Game of Thrones to its pivotal third season episode, “The Rains of Castamere”, comparing online threads by viewers familiar with the plot from the books on which the series is based with those of new fans who had not read the books.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) The paper analyzes online fan discussions of Game of Thrones' “Red Wedding” episode by comparing reactions between book-readers and show-only viewers to understand how different types of fans navigate transmedia storytelling systems.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) This study uses a comparative discourse analysis of fan responses to a dramatic Game of Thrones episode to explore how transmedia systems, like Game of Thrones, challenge fans to navigate complex networks of intertextualities, offering insights into the emerging model of the “transmedia fan” as both consumer and information-user in postdigital society.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) This article presents a comparative discourse analysis of online fan responses to Game of Thrones, examining how fans negotiate complex intertextualities and paratexts within the show's transmedia system.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) This study uses a comparative discourse analysis of fan responses to a specific episode of Game of Thrones to explore the behavior of “transmedia fans” and their navigation of a complex network of intertextual and paratextual materials across multiple platforms.
