# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Charles_L%27Enfant
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Pierre Charles L'Enfant was a French-born American revolutionary who served in the Continental Army during the Revolutionary War followed by a career in civil engineering which included redesigning New York's city hall and designing the basic plan for Washington, D.C. (the L'Enfant Plan).
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) French-born American military engineer Pierre Charles L'Enfant served in the Continental Army during the Revolutionary War, established a successful engineering firm in New York, and designed the famous L'Enfant Plan for Washington, D.C. in 1791.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) Pierre Charles L'Enfant, born in Paris in 1754, became a notable French-American military engineer who designed the L'Enfant Plan for Washington, D.C., served in the American Revolutionary War, redesigned New York City's Federal Hall, and created the insignia for the Society of the Cincinnati, marking his influential role in early American history.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Pierre Charles L'Enfant was a French-born American military engineer who designed the basic plan for Washington, D.C., and later established a successful civil engineering firm in New York City, where he achieved fame as an architect and designed various objects for the wealthy and government institutions.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Pierre Charles L'Enfant, a French-born American military engineer and urban planner, played a crucial role in designing the layout of Washington, D.C., and had a distinguished career in the American Revolutionary War and post-war architectural and engineering projects.
