# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Gordon
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Patrick Gordon was a 17th Century General and Rear Admiral of Scottish origin in the Russian Army, who was also a close friend and advisor of Tsar Peter the Great.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Patrick Gordon, a Scottish-born general and rear admiral who served in Sweden, Poland, and Russia, rose from trooper to full general and became a key advisor to Tsar Peter the Great, helping modernize the Russian army and establish the first Catholic church in Moscow.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) Patrick Leopold Gordon of Auchleuchries, born in 1635, was a Scottish soldier who served in Sweden and Poland, rose to be a general and advisor to Tsar Peter the Great and despite initial reluctance, was pivotal in the 1689 Moscow revolution, after which he secured a Catholic church and school in Russia as detailed in a diary up to his death in 1699.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) Patrick Gordon, a Scottish general and rear admiral, served in various European armies, including Sweden, Poland, and Russia, before becoming a close friend and advisor to Tsar Peter the Great, and kept a unique and detailed diary of his life.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Scottish general and admiral Patrick Gordon served multiple European countries including Sweden, Poland, and Russia, eventually becoming a trusted advisor to Tsar Peter the Great and achieving the rank of full general.
# meta::title = Patrick Gordon
