# meta::salientEntities = 1 (5*), 16 (5*), 141 (5*), 7 (4*), 9 (4*), 10 (4*), 17 (4), 2 (3*), 143 (3*), 13 (2*), 15 (2), 19 (2*), 105 (2), 106 (2), 75 (1*), 97 (1), 103 (1), 124 (1), 145 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ichiy%C5%8D_Higuchi
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Ichiyō Higuchi (pseudonym for Higuchi Natsu) was a Meiji era Japanese short story author, known for works such as Umoregi ("In Obscurity"), who, despite dying of tuberculosis at 24, became influential in Japanese literature and was commemorated on the Japanese 5,000 yen banknote.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Ichiyō Higuchi, Japan's first prominent modern female writer during the Meiji period, authored influential short stories like “Takekurabe” and “The Thirteenth Night” before dying of tuberculosis at age 24, later becoming honored on Japan's 5000 yen banknote.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) Ichiyo Higuchi, pen name of Natsu Higuchi, was a pivotal Meiji-era Japanese author known for her stories addressing social issues and women's plights, whose contributions to literature are celebrated despite her dying at 24 from tuberculosis, with her image on the 5000 yen banknote since 2004.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Ichiyō Higuchi, a Japanese author, wrote influential short stories that had a significant impact on Japanese literature, despite her brief life and early death at 24 due to tuberculosis.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Japanese author Natsu Higuchi, writing under the pen name Ichiyō Higuchi, produced impactful short stories during the Meiji period despite her tragically short life, exploring themes of loneliness, social class, and the struggles of women while gaining recognition late in her career before succumbing to tuberculosis at age 24.
