# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodorus_of_Tabennese
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Theodorus of Tabennese, also known as Abba Theodorus and Theodore the Sanctified was a 4th Century Christian monastic leader at the monastery in Tabennese, Egypt, who became the spiritual successor to the previous leader, Pachomius, and played a crucial role in preventing the first Christian cenobitic monastic federation from collapsing after his death.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Theodorus of Tabennese (314-368), a wealthy-born monk and spiritual successor to Pachomius, led the first Christian cenobitic monastic federation through various roles and challenges, eventually becoming its de facto leader for 18 years despite earlier setbacks.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) Initially denied joining due to his wealth, Theodorus of Tabennese became a key figure in stabilizing the Koinonia monastic federation after a leadership crisis, acting as a peacemaker among rebellious monks and leading the community effectively for eighteen years, before predicting his own death and peacefully passing authority to Horsiesios.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Theodorus of Tabennese, also known as Abba Theodorus, was a spiritual successor to Pachomius and played a crucial role in maintaining the first Christian cenobitic monastic federation after Pachomius' death, eventually becoming the de facto leader of the federation for nearly two decades.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Theodorus of Tabennese, a wealthy but pious young man who joined the monastic community founded by Pachomius, rose to become a key leader and ultimately prevented the collapse of the Pachomian monastic federation after Pachomius' death through his assertive governance and manipulation of power dynamics within the monasteries.
# meta::title = Theodorus of Tabennese
