# meta::genre = court
# meta::salientEntities = 4 (5*), 26 (5*), 34 (5*), 41 (4*), 93 (4*), 9 (3), 23 (3), 48 (3*), 58 (3*), 94 (3*), 39 (2), 40 (2), 99 (2*), 102 (2), 8 (1*), 33 (1*), 36 (1), 37 (1), 44 (1), 47 (1), 51 (1*), 68 (1), 70 (1), 75 (1*), 85 (1*), 88 (1*), 103 (1), 108 (1), 109 (1), 122 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://www.azcourts.gov/coa1/OralArguments/OralArgumentRecordings
# meta::speakerCount = 3
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Amy Sells, representing appellants Chase and Chelsea McCombs, argues that Debbie did not die in the fire at her house because she stayed to fight it and couldn't leave through the front door, which was double deadbolt locked in violation of statute, but, based on burns consistent with some furniture, may have fallen into a burning chair on an alternative escape route.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) In a case involving a fatal house fire, attorney Amy Sells argues that newly discovered photos showing burn patterns suggest the victim was trying to escape rather than fighting the fire, challenging the defense's intervening cause theory that claimed she stayed to combat the blaze despite statutory violations regarding smoke alarms and door locks.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) In a case concerning a house fire, attorneys debated the cause, whether an electrical fault or cigarette started it, and defense strategies blaming the victim for trying to extinguish it herself which allegedly led to her death due to smoke inhalation hindered by a deadbolted exit.
# meta::summary4 = (Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct) A lawyer, Amy Sells, argues that the origin of the fire is relevant to intervening superseding cause, specifically whether Debbie started the fire with a cigarette or an electrical spark, and that her death was caused by smoke inhalation due to delayed exit caused by a deadbolt on the front door.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; postedited) This court case revolves around whether the origin of a fire and the location where the victim fell are relevant to determining if her decision to stay and fight the fire constitutes an intervening, superseding cause of her death, given evidence suggesting she may have fallen while escaping rather than staying to fight the fire.
# meta::title = Arizona Court of Appeals McCombs v. Miller - Case No. 22-0634
