# newdoc id = GUM_essay_distraction
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Cory Doctorow
# meta::dateCollected = 2023-07-25
# meta::dateCreated = 2019-09-16
# meta::dateModified = 2019-09-16
# meta::genre = essay
# meta::salientEntities = 4 (5*), 63 (5*), 3 (4*), 92 (4*), 93 (4*), 20 (3*), 21 (3), 135 (3*), 32 (2*), 46 (2*), 128 (2*), 140 (2), 65 (1), 74 (1*), 94 (1), 95 (1), 112 (1), 141 (1*), 143 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://openwa.pressbooks.pub/lwtech88readings/
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Author Cory Doctorow gives advice on how to be productive and get writing done despite distractions from the Internet such as IM or email by keeping a short, regular work schedule of at least 20 minutes of work daily, leaving oneself a 'rough edge' of unfinished work to resume the next day, not expecting a perfect work environment, and turning off realtime communication tools.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Cory Doctorow argues that while the internet can be distracting, writers can successfully manage their online time and creative work through simple techniques like maintaining a short daily writing schedule, leaving unfinished edges in their work, avoiding ceremony, and eliminating real-time communication tools.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) Cory Doctorow argues that rather than avoiding the Internet, writers should embrace it and manage distractions through techniques like short, consistent writing sessions, setting daily goals, avoiding real-time communication interruptions, and not waiting for the perfect environment, to maintain productivity and creativity.
# meta::summary4 = (Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct) Author Cory Doctorow shares personal techniques for balancing writing with distractions, emphasizing the importance of setting a regular work schedule, leaving a rough edge, and avoiding real-time communications tools to maintain focus.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) To combat distractions and maintain productivity as a writer in the digital age, establish a consistent, limited work schedule, create a "rough edge" by stopping mid-sentence each day, and minimize interruptions from technology.
# meta::title = Writing in the Age of Distraction (in: 88 Open Essays - A Reader for Students of Composition & Rhetoric, Sarah Wangler & Tina Ulrich eds.)
