# meta::sourceURL = http://thoughtcrime.crummy.com/2009/Error.html
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Tyler, who always felt guilty for making his grandmother cry at their kitchen table when he told her to go to hell at age 5, discovers while looking at a photo album that they did not have a kitchen table, leading him to believe that his entire memory of the incident was false.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) A man grapples with a painful childhood memory of making his beloved grandmother cry, only to discover years later that the memory was fabricated, leaving him with mixed feelings of relief and loss.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) Tyler reflects on his deep childhood love for his grandmother and the guilt of a false memory in which he thought he hurt her, eventually realizing he had manufactured the memory due to feelings of abandonment and guilt, leading to a bittersweet sense of relief and loss when he discovers the truth through an old family photo.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Tyler, a young man, revisits a painful childhood memory of his grandmother's reaction to his cruelty as a child, but discovers that the memory is false and manufactured by his own imagination, leading to a newfound understanding and appreciation of his grandmother's love.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; postedited) Tyler, influenced by his grandmother's stories and visits to church, grapples with the guilt of a fabricated memory of yelling “Go to Hell” at her before discovering the memory was a product of his imagination after her death, and to top it off, he feels a bit sad knowing that a part of his love for her has died.
