# meta::genre = fiction
# meta::salientEntities = 2 (5*), 3 (5*), 73 (5*), 51 (4*), 64 (4*), 6 (3), 92 (3), 19 (2*), 80 (2*), 89 (2), 4 (1), 11 (1*), 59 (1*), 75 (1), 81 (1), 85 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/What_the_Moon_Brings
# meta::speakerCount = 1
# meta::summary1 = (human1) A protagonist tells of a terrifying walk on a moonlit summer night into a stream flowing to an ocean filled with the dead, during which face-like lotos blossoms whispered to him and a dead city of spires appeared in the sea, where sea worms feasted on flesh under an evil moon and a shocking figure with hooves and a crown gazed at him.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) In a surreal nightmare, a person wanders through an endless moonlit garden that leads to a vast sea, where they discover an ancient submerged city of the dead and flee into worm-infested waters to escape a horrifying creature emerging from the depths.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) A narrator, terrified of the moon and its ability to transform familiar places into nightmarish landscapes, finds himself in a spectral garden where moonlit waters reveal visions of a sunken, corpse-filled city and a monstrous entity rising from the depths, prompting him to flee into the ocean's grim recesses.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) The narrator is haunted by the moon's influence, which brings him visions of a dead and decaying city, and he flees into the sea to escape the horrors that lurk beneath its surface.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) The narrator wanders through an eerie, moonlit garden and discovers a sunken, decayed city filled with ghostly lotus faces and a monstrous eikon, feeling both entranced and terrified by the unearthly sights and smells.
