# meta::salientEntities = 1 (5*), 3 (5*), 2 (4), 7 (4), 6 (3), 40 (3*), 60 (2*), 61 (2*), 91 (2*), 92 (2*), 5 (1), 9 (1), 10 (1), 11 (1), 14 (1*), 39 (1*), 47 (1*), 49 (1*), 63 (1*), 66 (1), 67 (1), 102 (1*), 112 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = http://smallbeerpress.com/wp-content/uploads/The_Chemical_Wedding_sample.pdf
# meta::speakerCount = 4
# meta::summary1 = (human1) In a story whose history is explained by a brief introduction, Christian Rosencreutz is startled by a gust of wind and beautiful woman in a blue robe while baking a wafer for Easter Sunday, then three days later, he goes to a fountain with a lion statue and an inscription urging to drink and wash using the fountain's water.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) A mysterious and ancient text called “The Chemical Wedding” by Johann Valentin Andreae follows a man named Christian Rosencreutz who experiences strange events before Easter, including an encounter with a beautiful woman in sky-colored robes and a mystical journey to a lion fountain.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) The Chemical Wedding, purportedly authored by Christian Rosencreutz but actually written by Johann Valentin Andreae, is a surreal allegory of spiritual enlightenment framed around an eight-day mystical journey, blending elements of alchemy and the Christian narrative of death and resurrection, as a man named Christian experiences bizarre events unfolding before Easter.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) A new edition of “The Chemical Wedding” by Johann Valentin Andreae, attributed to Christian Rosencreutz, presents itself as a romance in eight days through John Crowley's new version and Theo Fadel's illustration, published by Small Beer Press.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) The Chemical Wedding describes the journey of Christian Rosencreutz, who embarks on a mystical eight-day quest filled with symbolic encounters and transformations, as depicted in a romanticized narrative by Johann Valentin Andreae.
# meta::title = The Chemical Wedding by Christian Rosencreutz
