# newdoc id = GUM_interview_herrick
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Wikinews
# meta::dateCollected = 2014-09-15
# meta::dateCreated = 2009-01-31
# meta::dateModified = 2010-07-01
# meta::genre = interview
# meta::salientEntities = 2 (5*), 3 (5*), 1 (4), 11 (4), 16 (4*), 17 (4*), 95 (4*), 114 (4*), 4 (3*), 20 (3), 37 (2), 43 (2), 12 (1), 21 (1), 22 (1), 29 (1), 46 (1), 47 (1*), 58 (1), 80 (1), 92 (1*), 128 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/Jack_Herrick,_wikiHow_founder_interviewed_by_Wikinews
# meta::speakerCount = 2
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Jack Herrick, founder of the how-to guide website wikiHow, talks to Wikinews about the success of his website, how it differs from his previous venture, eHow, and the reasons why he chose a Creative Commons license for wikiHow, which allows contributors to retain ownership of their content.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Wikinews interviews Jack Herrick, founder of the collaborative how-to website wikiHow, about its growth to over 14 million monthly visitors, its mission to build the world's largest how-to manual, and his decision to use Creative Commons licensing.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) In a Wikinews interview, Jack Herrick, wikiHow's founder, discusses its mission to be the largest how-to manual, the use of a Creative Commons license for content forking, its growth to 50,000 articles and 14 million monthly visitors, and the pros and cons of operating as a hybrid organization focused on social good.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) wikiHow founder Jack Herrick discusses the site's goal of creating a universal how-to manual, its growth to over 50,000 articles and 1,000 featured articles, and its use of a Creative Commons license to foster a community that can fork the site if it strays from its mission.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Jack Herrick, founder of wikiHow, discusses the vision behind creating a comprehensive how-to manual accessible to everyone, the challenges faced in implementing a free and open platform, and the growth of the site into a widely-read resource through collaboration and continuous improvement.
