# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/Wikinews_interviews_academic_Simon_Li%C4%8Den_about_attitudes_towards_US_Paralympics
# meta::speakerCount = 2
# meta::summary1 = (human1) In an interview with Wikinews, Simon Ličen, Assistant Professor in Educational Leadership, Sport Studies and Psychology at Washington State University, talks about the upcoming 2014 Winter Paralympics in Sochi, Russia, as well as the reasons for negative attitudes to the paralympics and challenges in media coverage for paralympics.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) A sports media researcher discusses the lack of visibility and challenges of Paralympic sports in the US compared to other countries and the potential impact of NBC's first live Paralympic broadcast during the 2014 Winter Games in Sochi.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) Wikinews interviews WSU Assistant Professor Simon Ličen on the low visibility of the Paralympic movement in the US compared to nations like Australia and the UK, discussing societal power dynamics, media representation, and the potential impact of the 2014 Sochi Winter Paralympics being televised live in the US for the first time.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Academic Simon Ličen discusses the limited visibility of the Paralympic movement in the US, citing factors such as the emphasis on consumerism and traditional notions of success, and the potential benefits and challenges of broadcasting the Paralympics live in the US for the first time.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Assistant Professor Simon Ličen discusses why the Paralympic movement has less visibility in the United States compared to other countries, and speculates on how the upcoming telecast of the 2014 Winter Paralympics in Sochi might impact public perception and media coverage in the US.
