# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/Keep_your_eyes_peeled_for_cosmic_debris:_Andrew_Westphal_about_Stardust@home
# meta::speakerCount = 2
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Wikinews interviews Andrew Westphal, director of NASA's Stardust@home project, which allows users to inspect vast amounts of data using a Virtual Microscope in their browser for interstellar dust particles, collected from comet 81P/Wild by NASA's Stardust space capsule.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Director Andrew Westphal discusses the NASA mission Stardust@home project, which needs human volunteers to analyze interstellar dust particles collected by the Stardust space capsule in 2006 using an online virtual microscope.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) Andrew Westphal, director of the Stardust@home project, discusses the NASA mission Stardust's reliance on volunteers using a “Virtual Microscope” to find tiny dust particles from comet Wild 2 and interstellar space, highlighting the challenges and uncertainties in predicting the exact number of particles expected to be found.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) Andrew Westphal, director of Stardust@home, discusses the project's goal of analyzing samples from comet 81P/Wild, which were collected by the Stardust space capsule and are being analyzed by volunteers using a virtual microscope.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; postedited) The Stardust@home project involves volunteers using a virtual microscope to search through images of aerogel collected by the Stardust spacecraft to find microscopic samples of interstellar dust, with the goal of advancing scientific understanding of the galaxy.
