# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/Texas_student_Ahmed_Mohamed_inspires_social_movement
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Ahmed Mohamed, a 14 year old muslim boy from Irving, Texas, was wrongfully arrested at school on September 14, 2015 by local police after a digital clock he had built was mistaken for a bomb, sparking protests on social media and leading to invitations for Ahmed to visit the White House, U.N. headquarters in New York, Facebook and Google Science Fair.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) 14-year-old Ahmed Mohamed's wrongful arrest for bringing a homemade digital clock to his Texas school sparked a global social movement, earning him support from President Obama and tech leaders, and invitations to the White House, Google, and UN.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) The arrest of 14-year-old Ahmed Mohamed for bringing a homemade clock to school, mistaken as a bomb, highlighted race and Islamophobia issues and sparked a global movement, leading to support for him from influential figures like President Obama and Mark Zuckerberg, and invitations to events like the Google Science Fair and a UN meeting.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) Ahmed Mohamed, a 14-year-old boy from Texas, was arrested and questioned by police after bringing a digital clock he made to school, but his case has sparked a global social movement in support of him, with many notable figures and organizations expressing their support and solidarity.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; postedited) A 14-year-old boy named Ahmed Mohamed faced arrest after bringing a homemade clock to school, but gained widespread support leading to meetings with tech leaders, the President of the United States, and international dignitaries and the spread of the hashtag #IStandWithAhmed.
