# newdoc id = GUM_news_election
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Wikinews
# meta::dateCollected = 2016-09-19
# meta::dateCreated = 2016-09-09
# meta::dateModified = 2016-09-10
# meta::genre = news
# meta::salientEntities = 1 (5*), 3 (5*), 4 (5*), 12 (5*), 2 (3), 6 (3*), 8 (3), 9 (3), 13 (3*), 24 (3), 16 (2*), 26 (2), 83 (2), 5 (1), 7 (1), 10 (1*), 14 (1), 15 (1), 17 (1), 23 (1), 25 (1), 76 (1), 85 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/New_faces_emerge_as_veteran_politicians_step_down_in_Hong_Kong_legislative_election
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) In the 2016 Hong Kong legislative election with a historically high turnout of 58%, the anti-establishment camp maintained its majority and veto powers, as a range of young and new candidates were elected across multiple constituencies.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) In Hong Kong's legislative election, a record 2 million voters participated, leading to significant victories for anti-establishment candidates and new faces while veteran politicians stepped down, with notable winners including Eddie Chu, Nathan Law, and Regina Ip.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) The Hong Kong legislative election on September 4, 2016, saw a record turnout of 2 million voters and resulted in the anti-establishment camp keeping its majority in geographical constituencies, with veteran politicians replaced by younger figures like nonpartisan pan-democrat Eddie Chu and Nathan Law, the youngest Legislative Council member.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) On September 9, 2016 it was reported that in the Hong Kong legislative election, a record-breaking turnout of around 2 million voters participated, with 58% of the eligible population casting their ballots, resulting in a majority of seats for the anti-establishment camp and a significant number of new faces emerging in the new council members.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) The Hong Kong legislative election saw high voter turnout and resulted in the anti-establishment camp maintaining a majority in geographical constituencies' seats, giving them veto power over certain bills, while veteran politicians from both the pro-Beijing and anti-establishment camps retired or were defeated, making way for younger and new faces in the council.
