# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/Scientists_analyse_effects_of_global_warming,_atmospheric_ozone_on_crops
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) In a study published on July 27, 2014 in the journal Nature Climate Change, scientists have forecast a substantial reduction of wheat, rice, maize and soybean crop yields by 2050 due to climate change and ozone pollution, leading to global undernourishment levels of 27–49%.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) A new study shows global warming and ozone pollution could reduce crop yields by over 10% by 2050, with ozone causing 46% of previously heat-attributed damage to soybeans, though pollution control measures could help mitigate these effects.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) A study in Nature Climate Change by MIT, Colorado State University, and the University of Sheffield found that from 2000 to 2050, climate-induced reductions in crop yields could exceed 10%, exacerbating food insecurity, with ozone pollution and temperature impacts varying by crop and region, highlighting the need for pollution reduction to improve agricultural outcomes.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) A study published in Nature Climate Change analyzed the effects of global warming and ozone pollution on crop yields worldwide from 2000 to 2050, finding that crop yields would decrease by 10% or more by 2050, affecting food security and potentially increasing undernourishment.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Scientists predict that by 2050, global warming and ozone pollution could reduce crop yields by over 10% compared to 2000 levels, threatening food security and potentially increasing undernourishment by up to 49%.
# meta::title = Scientists analyse effects of global warming, atmospheric ozone on crops
