# meta::sourceURL = https://openstax.org/details/books/us-history
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This section summarizes the history of Native American civilizations before the arrival of the Spanish, including the settlement of America over the Beringia land bridge between 9,000 and 15,000 years ago, the Olmecs on the Gulf Coast of Mexico (1200–400 BCE), and the Mayas (2000 BCE–900 CE) around Mexico, Belize, Honduras and Guatemala.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022; postedited) The section traces the early settlement of the Americas through the Bering land bridge and discusses major pre-Columbian civilizations, particularly the Olmec who established Mesoamerican culture and the Maya who made significant mathematical and architectural contributions before declining around 900 CE.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) The excerpt examines pre-Spanish American civilizations, emphasizing the Olmecs' foundational role in trade, religion, and writing, and the Maya's architectural and mathematical advancements, noting their decline from environmental challenges and the lasting influence of these cultures on later groups like the Aztecs.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) The Americas before 1492 were home to various civilizations, including the Olmec, Maya, Aztecs, and others, which developed complex cultures, agricultural systems, and writing systems, such as the Maya's calendar and written language.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) The Olmec, Maya, and other ancient civilizations in Mesoamerica developed complex cultures, advanced mathematics and astronomy, sophisticated architecture, and written languages prior to European contact, with the Olmec serving as the foundation for later cultures like the Maya and Aztec.
