# meta::salientEntities = 9 (5*), 31 (5*), 33 (5*), 107 (5*), 1 (4), 3 (3), 23 (3*), 25 (2), 86 (2*), 6 (1*), 13 (1), 16 (1), 46 (1), 49 (1), 65 (1*), 66 (1*), 67 (1*), 68 (1), 114 (1), 128 (1*), 130 (1), 131 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Thailand
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This article explains how and when to perform the respectful Wai hand gesture as a tourist in Thailand, how to dress appropriately at beaches and temples (e.g. taking off shoes at temples and homes), and how to interact with monks, including women not touching or handing things to them and not offering money to Theravada Buddhist monks.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Thai customs and etiquette emphasize respect through traditional practices like the wai greeting, conservative dress codes (especially at temples and sacred sites), and specific rules for interacting with Buddhist monks, who should not be touched by women or handle money directly.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) The article emphasizes respecting Thai culture, including the wai greeting, modest dress, and etiquette with Buddhist monks, advising visitors to be mindful of these practices to gain respect and avoid offense, while acknowledging the influence of Hindu culture and local dress norms.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) To respect Thai customs and culture, visitors should dress modestly, avoid revealing clothing, and follow traditional greetings and etiquette, such as bowing and not touching monks, to avoid causing offense and to show respect.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; postedited) In Thailand, showing respect through proper attire, adherence to social hierarchies, and appropriate interactions with monks is essential, so visitors should learn basic greetings like the wai and dress conservatively, especially when visiting temples.
