# meta::salientEntities = 1 (5*), 6 (5*), 10 (3), 14 (3*), 36 (3*), 96 (3*), 18 (2*), 81 (2), 93 (2), 127 (2*), 2 (1*), 13 (1*), 15 (1*), 17 (1), 32 (1), 37 (1), 44 (1), 48 (1*), 55 (1), 76 (1), 77 (1*), 82 (1), 91 (1), 111 (1*), 112 (1*), 114 (1*), 122 (1*), 124 (1), 125 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = http://www.wikihow.com/Detect-Arrogant-People
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This guide tells how to detect arrogant people based on signs such as irritation when they are not the center of attention, a strong need to look good and getting mad when their 'perfect world' is threatened, sudden hatefulness to friends or a cruel side shown to those they don't like, and speaking badly about you behind your back.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) This guide to detecting arrogant people explains key signs of arrogance including self-centered conversations, limited worldviews, need to look good, angry reactions to challenges, inability to handle ambiguity, fair-weather friendships, intolerance of differences, and speaking badly about others behind their backs.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) This article provides guidance on identifying arrogant people by suggesting paying attention to their self-centered conversations, intolerance towards differing views, and their treatment of relationships, which often include fair-weather friendships and a strong need to control their environment to protect their self-image and fantasy worldview.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) This article provides guidance on detecting and dealing with arrogant people, including recognizing their behaviors, such as being overly self-centered, dismissive of others' opinions, and critical of those they perceive as threats to their ego.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) This article provides tips for identifying arrogant individuals through their conversation patterns, need to control situations, jealousy, and treatment of others, while emphasizing the importance of observing their behavior and interactions rather than relying solely on direct confrontations.
# meta::title = How to Detect Arrogant People
