# newdoc id = GUM_whow_chicken
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Alex, Laura0681, Daniel Bauwens and others (see URL)
# meta::dateCollected = 2015-09-21
# meta::dateCreated = 2007-05-15
# meta::dateModified = 2015-07-14
# meta::genre = whow
# meta::salientEntities = 1 (5*), 2 (5*), 28 (5*), 33 (5*), 24 (4*), 43 (4), 44 (4*), 16 (2*), 11 (1), 12 (1*), 15 (1), 17 (1*), 18 (1*), 22 (1*), 23 (1*), 27 (1*), 40 (1*), 41 (1), 45 (1), 69 (1), 73 (1), 74 (1), 75 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://www.wikihow.com/Hypnotize-a-Chicken
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This guide gives instructions for 'hypnotizing' a chicken, by holding it with one hand with its breast on a flat surface and its legs held back, and then wiggling a finger from above the tip of its beak to its back or drawing a line before its beak until it stops squawking, resulting in a temporary attempt to 'play dead', which can be ended by clapping hands.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) A guide describes how to induce “tonic immobility” in chickens by holding them down and moving a finger or drawing a line in front of their beak, though this fear response should be used sparingly to avoid stressing the animals.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) This article explains how to hypnotize a chicken by holding it on a flat surface, wiggling a finger or drawing a line in front of its beak, and emphasizes the importance of reducing stress for the chicken, as prolonged or frequent hypnosis can cause health issues, although the phenomenon, known as “tonic immobility,” is generally considered safe when practiced briefly.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) Researchers have found that chickens can be hypnotized, and that this can be achieved through various methods, including holding the chicken down or drawing a line in front of its beak, while reducing stress through gentle handling and minimizing exposure to stressful situations.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) This article describes two methods for inducing a trance-like state in chickens, known as “tonic immobility,” where they become temporarily paralyzed due to perceived threat, including a simple finger-wiggling technique and drawing a line in front of their beak, while emphasizing the importance of minimizing stress and promoting a calm environment for the birds.
# meta::title = How to Hypnotize a Chicken
