# meta::salientEntities = 4 (5*), 27 (5*), 26 (4*), 48 (4*), 37 (2*), 88 (2*), 169 (2), 5 (1*), 6 (1), 13 (1*), 19 (1*), 35 (1), 59 (1), 71 (1), 73 (1), 86 (1), 89 (1), 91 (1), 104 (1*), 133 (1*), 136 (1*), 137 (1*), 138 (1*), 155 (1), 173 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://www.wikihow.com/Create-a-Language
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This language creation guide excerpt suggests naming your language and possibly basing it on an existing language, starting with frequent, short function words, making grammar rules and affixes for gender, using non-Latin characters and punctuation, writing texts and teaching it to friends, as well as giving certain letters positive/negative connotations.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) This guide to creating a constructed language outlines key steps like naming your language, establishing basic vocabulary and grammar rules, drawing inspiration from existing languages, developing writing systems, documenting everything, and sharing it with others.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) To create a made-up language, start by naming it and choosing basic words, establish grammar, possibly inspired by real languages, and use non-Latin characters if desired, then document the language and practice regularly to gain fluency, while being creative but avoiding gibberish, and considering expanding it to books or teaching friends and family.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct) To create a constructed language, start by selecting a name, defining grammatical rules, and designing a writing system, while ensuring consistency across words and tracing their etymology to establish authenticity.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) The article provides detailed guidance on creating a constructed language, including naming the language, choosing common words, establishing grammar rules, incorporating gender and plurality, basing vocabulary on existing languages or words, utilizing unique alphabets, and practicing the language through translation, teaching, and writing.
