# meta::sourceURL = https://www.wikihow.com/Prepare-Quinoa
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Three recipes for quinoa suggest optionally toasting it in olive oil in a pan and then cooking it in broth or water and salt on a stove, washing and cooking it in a rice cooker after optionally toasting it, or baking it in the oven in a baking dish covered with foil together with sauteed onions, peppers, mushrooms or other vegetables in a broth.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022; postedited) This comprehensive guide to preparing quinoa covers three cooking methods (stove top, rice cooker, and oven), detailing essential steps like rinsing, toasting for nutty flavor, using a 2:1 liquid-to-grain ratio, and cooking until the characteristic spiral germ appears.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o; postedited) This guide covers quinoa's history as a sacred Incan crop and offers cooking methods such as stove top, rice cooker, and oven, stressing rinsing to remove bitterness and proper water ratios, and suggests serving it with stir-fries, curries, and salads, highlighting its growing popularity, especially among vegetarians, for its high protein content.
# meta::summary4 = (Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct; postedited) This article provides a step-by-step guide on how to prepare quinoa, including three different methods: cooking on a stove, using a rice cooker, and baking in the oven, and covering instructions on rinsing and toasting the quinoa, cooking it with water or broth, and serving it with various ingredients.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; postedited) Quinoa, a nutritious Andean crop, can be prepared on a stove top, in a rice cooker, or in the oven by rinsing, toasting optionally, cooking until translucent with twice the volume of liquid, then letting it sit and fluffing, or alternatively, it can be sautéed with vegetables before adding liquid and cooking in the oven.
